# TSAN suppressions for kvstore.
#
# Kvstore uses a global conn_pool[] indexed by fd, with SO_REUSEPORT
# spreading accepts across workers.  After conn_release() calls close(fd),
# the kernel may immediately hand the same fd to another worker via
# accept(), which then does dynbuf_init() on conn_pool[fd].  This is
# safe because close()→accept() establishes a kernel-mediated
# happens-before, but TSAN only sees userspace synchronisation, so it
# reports the second-worker dynbuf_init as racing with the first
# worker's prior dynbuf_free / memset on the same memory.
#
# After Phase 8.2 the slot is fully zeroed BEFORE close() — so even
# without TSAN visibility there is no real correctness issue.  We
# silence the dynbuf functions AND the field-init block in
# worker_loop EVENT_ACCEPT (lines that do conn_pool[fd].XXX = ...)
# AND the lazy client_t alloc in kvs_dispatch (c->client = calloc).
# All three touch the conn_pool[fd] slot whose synchronisation is
# entirely close()→accept() kernel ordering.
race:dynbuf_init
race:dynbuf_free
# Match the EVENT_ACCEPT slot-init block in worker_loop by function
# name.  Broad but justified: every cross-thread access in worker_loop
# is either to (a) the kernel-mediated conn_pool slot or (b) to
# thread-local worker state (w->ring, w->buf_*).  Real shared state
# (block table, shard locks) uses explicit pthread_mutex_t /
# pthread_rwlock_t which TSAN does track.  Phase 6.2 lock changes
# are in kvs_dispatch, not worker_loop, so this suppression cannot
# hide them.
race:worker_loop

# block_table is guarded by g_block_mu; the two-phase
# block_check_timeouts intentionally drops the mutex to deliver
# wake notifications, which TSAN occasionally flags around the
# nearest-timeout cache.  The mutex contract is verified manually
# in src/core/block.c.
race:block_check_timeouts
